1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,640
Hello everyone, this is Ria!

2
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:09,200
Does my voice sound a bit strange today?

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:14,330
Isn't it a little different from usual?

4
00:00:14,330 --> 00:00:17,990
Actually, I caught a cold.

5
00:00:17,990 --> 00:00:19,300
Cold

6
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:23,970
It's summer now, but I caught a cold.

7
00:00:23,970 --> 00:00:31,970
That's why in the thumbnail, I was wrapped up in a blanket like this.

8
00:00:31,970 --> 00:00:36,170
I was wrapped up in a blanket.

9
00:00:36,170 --> 00:00:41,050
There is a famous Korean proverb.

10
00:00:41,050 --> 00:00:42,870
Proverb!

11
00:00:42,870 --> 00:00:47,780
A summer cold doesn't even catch a dog.

12
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:54,140
There is a saying that a summer cold doesn't even catch a dog.

13
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:56,700
Not even a dog catches it.

14
00:00:56,700 --> 00:01:02,120
The word '도' (also/even) is used for emphasis.

15
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:03,770
It was emphasized.

16
00:01:03,770 --> 00:01:12,280
So if not even a dog catches it, how can a person catch a summer cold?

17
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:14,230
That's what it means.

18
00:01:14,230 --> 00:01:19,910
Just by adding '도' the feeling changes.

19
00:01:19,910 --> 00:01:28,500
Isn't it nice to learn Korean proverbs like this for small talk?

20
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:36,340
If you catch a cold in summer in Korea,

21
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:40,320
someone might ask you like this.

22
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:44,180
Ria, did you catch a cold?

23
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:54,400
Yes, they say a summer cold doesn't even catch a dog... but I caught a cold.

24
00:01:54,400 --> 00:02:00,220
If a foreigner said this to me,

25
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:08,800
I would think, "Ah, this person really knows Korean well," "Amazing."

26
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:13,390
Anyway, how does my voice sound, everyone?

27
00:02:13,390 --> 00:02:16,330
Nose, nose cold

28
00:02:16,330 --> 00:02:19,570
Throat, throat cold

29
00:02:19,570 --> 00:02:27,030
I caught a nose cold and throat cold, so my voice sounds a bit strange.

30
00:02:27,030 --> 00:02:31,560
My nose is a bit stuffy right now.

31
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:33,220
My nose is stuffy.

32
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:48,170
When your voice sounds strange because of a nose cold or throat cold, Koreans call that a "코맹맹이 소리" (nasal sound).

33
00:02:48,170 --> 00:02:50,420
Nasal sound.

34
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:59,610
If you block your nose and speak Korean now, your voice will sound like mine.

35
00:02:59,610 --> 00:03:03,080
This is the nasal sound.

36
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:06,800
It's a sound I really dislike.

37
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:13,240
Where do you hurt the most when you catch a cold?

38
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:16,250
Where is the weakest spot?

39
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:19,800
For me, my throat hurts first.

40
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:25,510
The symptoms always start from the throat.

41
00:03:25,510 --> 00:03:26,910
Symptoms

42
00:03:26,910 --> 00:03:31,850
My throat starts hurting first.

43
00:03:31,850 --> 00:03:40,750
My throat hurts first, then I cough a lot.

44
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:43,120
Cough

45
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:45,140
This kind of cough.

46
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:49,110
My body

47
00:03:49,110 --> 00:04:02,970
I have a job where I use my throat the most, but my throat is also the weakest part.

48
00:04:02,970 --> 00:04:05,860
It's a very sad thing.

49
00:04:05,860 --> 00:04:15,540
When I'm not feeling well or tired, my throat hurts first.

50
00:04:15,540 --> 00:04:22,160
So when my throat starts to feel a little sore,

51
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:26,550
I immediately think, "Oh no..."

52
00:04:26,550 --> 00:04:31,250
A cold is coming! No! Please...

53
00:04:31,250 --> 00:04:40,850
At that time, I take a lot of vitamins and rest a lot.

54
00:04:40,850 --> 00:04:45,850
But lately, I haven't been able to rest well.

55
00:04:45,850 --> 00:04:52,630
Actually, I'm not in Korea right now.

56
00:04:52,630 --> 00:04:54,010
Surprise news

57
00:04:54,010 --> 00:04:58,200
I'm in Japan right now.

58
00:04:58,200 --> 00:05:02,760
The background looks a bit different from usual, right?

59
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:08,870
The background where I film videos is a bit different from usual.

60
00:05:08,870 --> 00:05:12,480
I'm in Japan right now.

61
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:24,370
I went on a trip to Japan before, and this time I plan to travel in Japan for a while.

62
00:05:24,370 --> 00:05:33,000
But lately, I've been mostly working here.

63
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:38,270
There is no fan in this accommodation in Japan.

64
00:05:38,270 --> 00:05:44,650
It's summer in Japan now, so it's very hot.

65
00:05:44,650 --> 00:05:53,150
During the day, it's about 36 to 37 degrees Celsius.

66
00:05:53,150 --> 00:06:01,800
It's so hot, and since there is no fan in the room, I have to use the air conditioner.

67
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:08,470
I had no choice but to run the air conditioner all day.

68
00:06:08,470 --> 00:06:13,210
I even ran the air conditioner while sleeping.

69
00:06:13,210 --> 00:06:17,610
That's probably why I caught a cold.

70
00:06:17,610 --> 00:06:22,610
This is called air conditioning sickness.

71
00:06:22,610 --> 00:06:24,950
Air conditioning sickness

72
00:06:24,950 --> 00:06:30,900
A cold caught because of the cold air from the air conditioner.

73
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:32,980
Air conditioning sickness

74
00:06:32,980 --> 00:06:41,570
Or maybe I caught it from people while traveling.

75
00:06:41,570 --> 00:06:44,210
The pronunciation is a bit difficult, right?

76
00:06:44,210 --> 00:06:53,770
Catch a cold / catch a cold (present) / caught a cold (past)

77
00:06:53,770 --> 00:06:59,820
Yes, being sick overseas makes me even sadder.

78
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:03,600
Ah, I'm really upset.

79
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:05,390
I'm upset.

80
00:07:05,390 --> 00:07:12,300
I had a podcast I wanted to make this week.

81
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:20,490
It was an important podcast, but I didn't want to do it with this voice.

82
00:07:20,490 --> 00:07:27,060
I'll tell you what kind of podcast it is a little later.

83
00:07:27,060 --> 00:07:39,960
I had plans, but when I get sick and my body feels weak, all those plans get ruined.

84
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:45,050
That kind of thing stresses me out a lot.

85
00:07:45,050 --> 00:07:57,330
When things were going smoothly, and suddenly they get ruined, that's stressful.

86
00:07:57,330 --> 00:08:01,380
'착착' has many meanings.

87
00:08:01,380 --> 00:08:08,010
Here, '착착' means things are going well.

88
00:08:08,010 --> 00:08:16,470
It means things are progressing smoothly without any blockage.

89
00:08:16,470 --> 00:08:19,380
Think of a puzzle.

90
00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:28,360
Puzzle pieces fitting together one by one smoothly.

91
00:08:28,360 --> 00:08:42,340
My plans were going smoothly like that, but suddenly I caught a cold and I was very upset.

92
00:08:42,340 --> 00:08:48,890
Basically, '착착' is said twice.

93
00:08:48,890 --> 00:08:52,590
It's going well.

94
00:08:52,590 --> 00:09:07,060
But in daily conversation, when you want to express the feeling more strongly, you sometimes say it multiple times like '착착착착'.

95
00:09:07,060 --> 00:09:10,780
There is no fixed number.

96
00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:15,960
But if you say it too many times, it might sound strange, right?

97
00:09:15,960 --> 00:09:19,050
착착착착착착착...(hahaha)

98
00:09:19,050 --> 00:09:21,550
Short break!

99
00:09:21,550 --> 00:09:22,700
Water

100
00:09:22,700 --> 00:09:28,340
I'll drink some water.

101
00:09:30,390 --> 00:09:38,070
Actually, today's real podcast topic is not about colds.

102
00:09:38,070 --> 00:09:40,890
You were tricked, right?

103
00:09:40,890 --> 00:09:42,600
You were tricked, right?

104
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:47,670
Today, I'm going to talk about something really important.

105
00:09:47,670 --> 00:09:51,360
It's Korean spelling.

106
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:53,960
What is spelling?

107
00:09:53,960 --> 00:10:01,410
It's the correct way and rules of writing Hangul.

108
00:10:01,410 --> 00:10:11,180
For example, if I say "안녕하세요, 리아야요." that's wrong spelling.

109
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:15,930
You should write "리아예요."

110
00:10:15,930 --> 00:10:20,630
Anyway, I'm sick right now.

111
00:10:20,630 --> 00:10:23,220
I'm sick.

112
00:10:23,220 --> 00:10:25,890
I caught a cold.

113
00:10:25,890 --> 00:10:35,380
Then, what is the thing you most want to say to me right now?

114
00:10:35,380 --> 00:10:40,230
The thing you most want to say!

115
00:10:40,230 --> 00:10:48,640
When someone catches a cold, the phrase Koreans say the most!

116
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:51,630
Get well soon from your cold.

117
00:10:51,630 --> 00:10:54,870
Recover quickly from your cold.

118
00:10:54,870 --> 00:10:56,250
That's it.

119
00:10:56,250 --> 00:10:58,130
Recover.

120
00:10:58,130 --> 00:11:03,440
But there's a really interesting fact.

121
00:11:03,440 --> 00:11:11,770
Even Koreans sometimes make mistakes writing this phrase.

122
00:11:11,770 --> 00:11:20,190
I brought a picture from the Korean internet.

123
00:11:20,190 --> 00:11:30,630
The worst spelling mistake chosen by people in their 20s and 30s, number one: "빨리 낳으세요."

124
00:11:30,630 --> 00:11:33,300
It's number one.

125
00:11:33,300 --> 00:11:34,520
Number one.

126
00:11:34,520 --> 00:11:43,770
This is the spelling mistake Koreans hate the most.

127
00:11:43,770 --> 00:11:57,130
Even if we are Korean and Korean is our mother tongue, sometimes we make spelling mistakes.

128
00:11:57,130 --> 00:12:01,680
Those mistakes are understandable.

129
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:10,030
But the mistake "빨리 낳으세요." is really the worst.

130
00:12:10,030 --> 00:12:15,670
Let me tell you why it's the worst.

131
00:12:15,670 --> 00:12:20,600
There are two words here.

132
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:22,590
낳다 [nahta]

133
00:12:22,590 --> 00:12:24,590
낫다 [natta]

134
00:12:24,590 --> 00:12:26,370
낳다 [nahta]

135
00:12:26,370 --> 00:12:27,800
낫다 [natta]

136
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:35,730
Since this is a podcast, some of you might be listening only by ear.

137
00:12:35,730 --> 00:12:39,930
So it might be a bit confusing.

138
00:12:39,930 --> 00:12:46,190
Please try to watch the screen as much as possible today.

139
00:12:46,190 --> 00:12:49,560
Please watch while listening.

140
00:12:49,560 --> 00:12:55,430
But I'll try to explain a lot verbally.

141
00:12:55,430 --> 00:13:03,480
So you can understand by ear, I'll explain a lot by speaking.

142
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:11,800
The first '낳다' has a final consonant 'ㅎ' (h).

143
00:13:11,800 --> 00:13:20,370
The second '낫다' has a final consonant 'ㅅ' (s).

144
00:13:20,370 --> 00:13:27,830
Let's look at the first '낳다' with the 'ㅎ' final consonant.

145
00:13:27,830 --> 00:13:34,800
'낳다' means a mother giving birth to a baby.

146
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:36,990
To give birth to a baby.

147
00:13:36,990 --> 00:13:40,730
It means a baby is born.

148
00:13:40,730 --> 00:13:43,660
Here's something important.

149
00:13:43,660 --> 00:13:51,930
If you change it to '요' form, the pronunciation becomes '낳아요 [naayo]'.

150
00:13:51,930 --> 00:13:53,880
낳아요 [naayo]

151
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:57,240
I will give birth to a baby next year.

152
00:13:57,240 --> 00:14:08,920
When writing, the 'ㅎ' is included, but when speaking, the 'ㅎ' sound is not pronounced at all.

153
00:14:08,920 --> 00:14:11,130
I give birth to a baby.

154
00:14:11,130 --> 00:14:14,890
I will give birth to a baby next year.

155
00:14:14,890 --> 00:14:21,320
And second, "낫다. 낫다."

156
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:25,910
This one has a 'ㅅ' final consonant.

157
00:14:25,910 --> 00:14:32,730
This word has two common meanings.

158
00:14:32,730 --> 00:14:37,880
The first is 'to recover from a cold.'

159
00:14:37,880 --> 00:14:43,700
This is what you can say to me now.

160
00:14:43,700 --> 00:14:48,830
It means the cold is completely gone.

161
00:14:48,830 --> 00:14:51,600
Recover from a cold.

162
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:53,950
Recover from illness.

163
00:14:53,950 --> 00:14:59,210
The second meaning is like the English word 'better.'

164
00:14:59,210 --> 00:15:04,020
It means when talking about which is better.

165
00:15:04,020 --> 00:15:08,460
Which one is better between the two?

166
00:15:08,460 --> 00:15:14,900
Is this better? Or is that better? It has that meaning.

167
00:15:14,900 --> 00:15:21,910
If you change this word to '-요' form, it becomes '나아요.'

168
00:15:21,910 --> 00:15:25,020
낫다 - 나아요

169
00:15:25,020 --> 00:15:30,820
It sounds exactly the same as '아기 낳아요 [naayo]' (give birth to a baby).

170
00:15:30,820 --> 00:15:32,990
Exactly the same.

171
00:15:32,990 --> 00:15:37,900
That's why Koreans get confused.

172
00:15:37,900 --> 00:15:43,320
The spelling is different when writing in Hangul.

173
00:15:43,320 --> 00:15:48,640
But the pronunciation sounds the same.

174
00:15:48,640 --> 00:15:53,560
낳아요. 나아요.

175
00:15:53,560 --> 00:16:08,230
So if you write "감기 빨리 낳으세요." using the 'ㅎ' final consonant, it means giving birth to a baby.

176
00:16:08,230 --> 00:16:12,600
It's a really strange spelling mistake.

177
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:20,050
So in this picture, it says, "Stop giving birth to colds!!!"

178
00:16:20,050 --> 00:16:29,580
Colds are not babies, so why use '낳다' which is used for giving birth to babies!

179
00:16:29,580 --> 00:16:31,360
Second picture!

180
00:16:31,360 --> 00:16:33,650
This is funny too.

181
00:16:33,650 --> 00:16:41,160
The cold you told me to give birth to is our son’s cold.

182
00:16:41,160 --> 00:16:44,370
This is really funny, right?

183
00:16:44,370 --> 00:16:49,590
"No, when did I say that..." The man was embarrassed.

184
00:16:49,590 --> 00:16:51,640
He was embarrassed.

185
00:16:51,640 --> 00:16:52,970
Try hugging him.

186
00:16:52,970 --> 00:16:55,410
Our son’s cold.

187
00:16:55,410 --> 00:16:57,550
What do you think?

188
00:16:57,550 --> 00:17:02,290
Are you a bit confused listening to this?

189
00:17:02,290 --> 00:17:05,030
Or is it okay?

190
00:17:05,030 --> 00:17:08,590
Are you understanding well?

191
00:17:08,590 --> 00:17:21,490
Actually, even Koreans sometimes get confused when the spelling is different but the sound is the same.

192
00:17:21,490 --> 00:17:26,770
I want to give you a tip.

193
00:17:26,770 --> 00:17:35,730
When '낳다' has the 'ㅎ' final consonant, always think of a baby.

194
00:17:35,730 --> 00:17:40,760
Think of the 'ㅎ' shape as a baby.

195
00:17:40,760 --> 00:17:43,130
Baby face shape.

196
00:17:43,130 --> 00:17:44,740
Baby face.

197
00:17:44,740 --> 00:17:54,250
Then you will never write "감기 빨리 낳으세요." again, right?

198
00:17:54,250 --> 00:17:55,840
Never!

199
00:17:55,840 --> 00:18:05,210
If you know someone who is giving birth to a baby, then you can use it then.

200
00:18:05,210 --> 00:18:10,040
Have a healthy and safe delivery!

201
00:18:10,040 --> 00:18:17,360
In a cooler Korean way, this is called 'sunsan' (순산).

202
00:18:17,360 --> 00:18:18,740
Sunsan.

203
00:18:18,740 --> 00:18:20,870
Have a smooth delivery!

204
00:18:20,870 --> 00:18:22,410
It's a bit difficult, right?

205
00:18:22,410 --> 00:18:33,420
But when you're not talking about giving birth but a cold! When talking about a cold, the 'ㅎ' (hieut) disappears.

206
00:18:33,420 --> 00:18:36,680
Get well from your cold quickly!

207
00:18:36,680 --> 00:18:44,360
Do many people make spelling mistakes in language?

208
00:18:44,360 --> 00:18:48,820
What do you think when you see those people?

209
00:18:48,820 --> 00:18:57,810
Not just simple mistakes, but people who don't even know the very basics.

210
00:18:57,810 --> 00:19:01,750
What do you think when you see those people?

211
00:19:01,750 --> 00:19:12,850
Koreans say 'jeong tteoreojinda' (정떨어진다) when they see people who make spelling mistakes.

212
00:19:12,850 --> 00:19:14,230
Jeong.

213
00:19:14,230 --> 00:19:17,010
Jeong means heart.

214
00:19:17,010 --> 00:19:19,360
A deep heart.

215
00:19:19,360 --> 00:19:22,860
This heart falls away.

216
00:19:22,860 --> 00:19:24,360
Falls away.

217
00:19:24,360 --> 00:19:30,580
So it means the feeling of liking disappears.

218
00:19:30,580 --> 00:19:32,620
Jeong tteoreojida.

219
00:19:32,620 --> 00:19:35,720
This phrase is used a lot.

220
00:19:35,720 --> 00:19:40,290
In slang, it's shortened to 'jeong tteol' (정떨).

221
00:19:40,290 --> 00:19:46,540
"Oh, that guy is really jeong tteol." People say like this.

222
00:19:46,540 --> 00:19:49,790
Here's more slang!

223
00:19:49,790 --> 00:19:51,800
More slang.

224
00:19:51,800 --> 00:19:55,200
Shall I say it? Or not?

225
00:19:55,200 --> 00:19:58,650
There is a word 'gae jeong tteol' (개정떨).

226
00:19:58,650 --> 00:20:02,750
Here, 'gae' is not a curse word.

227
00:20:02,750 --> 00:20:12,240
Sometimes when 'gae' is in a word, some people think it's always a curse word.

228
00:20:12,240 --> 00:20:15,450
'Gae' is a word for emphasis.

229
00:20:15,450 --> 00:20:21,400
It emphasizes strongly, so it sounds a bit harsh.

230
00:20:21,400 --> 00:20:26,260
You only use it in front of really close friends.

231
00:20:26,260 --> 00:20:28,790
Ah, really gae jeong tteol!

232
00:20:28,790 --> 00:20:35,620
If you stretch the 'gae' a bit longer, it sounds even more real.

233
00:20:35,620 --> 00:20:37,140
Gae jeong tteol.

234
00:20:37,140 --> 00:20:39,670
Really gae jeong tteol.

235
00:20:39,670 --> 00:20:47,880
I thought many people would want to learn slang like this, so I mentioned it once.

236
00:20:47,880 --> 00:20:53,940
Everyone, I brought Korean news again today.

237
00:20:53,940 --> 00:20:59,570
The title is 'Broke up because of spelling mistakes.'

238
00:20:59,570 --> 00:21:00,800
Wow, amazing.

239
00:21:00,800 --> 00:21:09,080
It means they broke up because of losing affection due to spelling mistakes.

240
00:21:09,080 --> 00:21:18,290
I've never actually seen anyone confuse '낳다' (to give birth) and '낫다' (to get better).

241
00:21:18,290 --> 00:21:27,000
But if I actually saw it, I think it would be quite unpleasant.

242
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,900
There is also this kind of photo.

243
00:21:29,900 --> 00:21:34,660
Younghee, get well from your cold quickly.

244
00:21:34,660 --> 00:21:38,350
Yes, I will have a smooth delivery.

245
00:21:38,350 --> 00:21:41,590
I hope you meet a good person...

246
00:21:41,590 --> 00:21:44,670
We learned 'sunsan' earlier, right?

247
00:21:44,670 --> 00:21:49,830
This guy made a spelling mistake.

